{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1482664-instances-of-damage","id":1482664,"title":"Instances of Damage","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-06-01T14:53:25-07:00","id":1482664,"title":"Instances of Damage","content":"So, this is a hot topic between many players and ST alike. That is &quot;instances of damage&quot;<br /> Let's take for instance, a <i>Sword of Mars</i> (Forged by Dragon's Fire, pg. 59), it states: &quot;If the user rolls the sword's Arete of 2 with a difficulty of 5, it can also amplify the power of the wirlde's swing and add standard Foces damage to the blow.&quot; So, the discussion is as how is this damage applied. Does it simply increase the damage by the amount rolled or would the damage from Forces be considered another &quot;instance of damage&quot; and thus soaked in a different roll?<br /> <br /> The discussion usually happens with enchanted items, mostly weapons, said if you enchant a sword with fire, is the fire damage another &quot;instance&quot; and soaked with another roll or should it be simply added to the rest of the damage?<br /> <br /> Take notes that it makes a HUGE difference, one thing is to have to soak 2-3 fire damage from a fiery sword as a vampire and another is soaking 10 THAT IS CONSIDERED AS FIRE DAMAGE if one simply put it all together.<br /> <br /> I think there are different cases, the Sword of Mars, in my opinion, is simply added to the damage, but an enchanted sword would just adds the fire damage in a difference &quot;instance&quot;, so the vampire in question would have to soak the 2-3 fire damage separately from the 7-8 of the blow.<br /> I'd like to hear your opinion on the matter.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-01T15:36:22-07:00","id":1482673,"title":"","content":"The problem with treating them as distinct instances is twofold. First, it makes &quot;bonus&quot; to damage be less impressive if it isn't big. Second, it increases the number of rolls. While I get the appeal of increasing the relevance of Soaking in an already exceedingly deadly system, it seems weird and unfair, and too bulky to handle.<br /> <br /> But it doesn't mean that the whole damage must be treated equal just because it is pooled together. Lets say the fiery sword causes a total 7 damage to the vampire, 3 cutting, 4 fire. For the effect of <i>applying</i> soak and damage type I would just keep them separated. So the Vampire rolls Stamina + Fortitude, but Stamina successes beyond 3 are wasted since they can't soak Fire, you can even let Fortitude soak Fire first if you want, then apply whatever pass on to HL as they should: some is Lethal, some is Aggravated.<br /> <br /> While it may take some time to adjust, in my experience distinguishing the dice like that is actually pretty simple. You can distinguish them by color, position they fall, or simply roll separately each sub-pool, but still apply the effect in bulk.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-01T15:56:51-07:00","id":1482676,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1482664-instances-of-damage?p=1482673#post1482673\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The problem with treating them as distinct instances is twofold. First, it makes &quot;bonus&quot; to damage be less impressive if it isn't big. Second, it increases the number of rolls. While I get the appeal of increasing the relevance of Soaking in an already exceedingly deadly system, it seems weird and unfair, and too bulky to handle.<br /> <br /> But it doesn't mean that the whole damage must be treated equal just because it is pooled together. Lets say the fiery sword causes a total 7 damage to the vampire, 3 cutting, 4 fire. For the effect of <i>applying</i> soak and damage type I would just keep them separated. So the Vampire rolls Stamina + Fortitude, but Stamina successes beyond 3 are wasted since they can't soak Fire, you can even let Fortitude soak Fire first if you want, then apply whatever pass on to HL as they should: some is Lethal, some is Aggravated.<br /> <br /> While it may take some time to adjust, in my experience distinguishing the dice like that is actually pretty simple. You can distinguish them by color, position they fall, or simply roll separately each sub-pool, but still apply the effect in bulk.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> This seems to be the best approach in my opinion as well. I didn't think about it mostly because we don't roll soak against damage besides fire/sunlight and some other things. So an aggravated sword attack would be automatically soaked by the stamina + fortitude of the one soaking, while fire/sunlight, etc, we roll to soak, so all in all, we already have less rolls.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-01T18:01:44-07:00","id":1482689,"title":"","content":"I usually give my players the following choice:<br /> <br /> Either they can gain dice of damage, but the magic weapon's damage code is based on the magic in question, or you can have the weapon do a second set of magical damage that does levels of damage that triggers on the first mundane attack.<br /> <br /> So you can have a fiery sword that does a base of Str + 5 damage dice soaked as fire when activated, or a Str + 3 sword of normal lethal damage that then also does a fire damage spell for X levels of damage based on the roll.<br /> <br /> Both approaches have pros and cons and neither really tends to out perform the other.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-01T18:19:48-07:00","id":1482692,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Both approaches have pros and cons and neither really tends to out perform the other. </div> </div> </div>But which one would you choose?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-01T18:31:27-07:00","id":1482695,"title":"","content":"Depends on my goals.<br /> <br /> I tend towards the first for more general weapons. The things my characters rely on by default. It's more &quot;reliable&quot; in a sense of less likely to run afoul of bad Arete rolls (since rolling Arete every hit means a magical botch gets much more likely than one Arete roll to get your weapon buffed for a duration if not a permanent one). I tend toward the second for weapons designed for specific foes in mind. If I want to keep out of close combat as much as possible (lets say because Garou going claw happy with Rage points to spend), having a weapon that uses Forces to increase the impact and send a foe flying away every hit might not do as much damage as making it Agg, but it means they've got to burn actions closing into hand-to-hand range again. Though Garou are a bit of a low bar because turning your weapon into silver is very easy for mages to exploit.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}